00:06:13.180 --> 00:06:16.311
So we should probably talk about
what Kubernetes actually is,

00:06:16.311 --> 00:06:17.810
and why people
should care about it,

00:06:17.810 --> 00:06:21.400
and why, basically, all three
of us think it's pretty awesome.

00:06:21.400 --> 00:06:24.340
So since you're the
guest on the show, Brian,

00:06:24.340 --> 00:06:25.480
why don't you take us away?

00:06:25.480 --> 00:06:26.480
BRIAN DORSEY: Ah, sweet.

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:32.540
So I think the big picture
thing is context-wise,

00:06:32.540 --> 00:06:36.870
more and more people have access
to lots and lots of computers.

00:06:36.870 --> 00:06:40.460
And most software today
is running across--

00:06:40.460 --> 00:06:43.767
is already a distributed system.

00:06:43.767 --> 00:06:46.100
You're running different
processes on multiple machines,

00:06:46.100 --> 00:06:48.720
and keeping track of
all this is a challenge.

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:51.180
And so, at a very
high level, Kubernetes

00:06:51.180 --> 00:06:55.200
helps you, basically, run
your software, your processes,

00:06:55.200 --> 00:06:58.750
on a whole cluster of computers,
but reason about all of them

00:06:58.750 --> 00:07:01.080
as one thing, as one entity.

00:07:01.080 --> 00:07:03.080
So you can say,
hey, I want to run

00:07:03.080 --> 00:07:04.890
five copies of this process.

00:07:04.890 --> 00:07:07.480
Kubernetes, make that happen
somewhere in the cluster.

00:07:07.480 --> 00:07:09.560
Make sure they stay running.

00:07:09.560 --> 00:07:11.090
If anything goes
wrong, let me know.

00:07:11.090 --> 00:07:12.147
Restart them.

00:07:12.147 --> 00:07:14.230
If one of the machines
goes down, reschedule them.

00:07:14.230 --> 00:07:15.424
That sort of thing.

00:07:15.424 --> 00:07:16.340
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:07:16.340 --> 00:07:18.160
So that actually
sounds a little bit

00:07:18.160 --> 00:07:20.975
like what we do here at
Google, a little bit.

00:07:20.975 --> 00:07:22.220
BRIAN DORSEY: A Little bit.

00:07:22.220 --> 00:07:23.870
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, like
having a lot of machines

00:07:23.870 --> 00:07:25.700
and trying to run
some stuff on it.

00:07:25.700 --> 00:07:29.270
So I heard a lot of
people saying, oh yeah,

00:07:29.270 --> 00:07:32.000
Kubernetes is just the
open source version

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:35.310
of what we do here at
Google, which is Borg.

00:07:35.310 --> 00:07:36.633
Is that true?

00:07:36.633 --> 00:07:39.600
BRIAN DORSEY: In the--
like, philosophically?

00:07:39.600 --> 00:07:40.150
Absolutely.

00:07:40.150 --> 00:07:43.240
So it's inspired by Borg.

00:07:43.240 --> 00:07:45.650
But it is a from the
ground up, actually,

00:07:45.650 --> 00:07:48.120
a more modern implementation
of a lot of these ideas

00:07:48.120 --> 00:07:49.222
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:07:49.222 --> 00:07:50.930
BRIAN DORSEY: Yeah,
and, in fact, there's

00:07:50.930 --> 00:07:53.940
a fantastic talk-- we can add
the links to the show notes--

00:07:53.940 --> 00:07:56.909
by John Wilkes, talking about
the relationship of Borg

00:07:56.909 --> 00:07:58.950
and what it looks like,
as a developer at Google,

00:07:58.950 --> 00:08:02.040
to write code and run
that on our clusters,

00:08:02.040 --> 00:08:04.530
and then what we've
built with Kubernetes.

00:08:04.530 --> 00:08:08.915
So it's a fun talk, diving
deep into that relationship.

00:08:08.915 --> 00:08:10.290
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Ah, that's cool.

00:08:10.290 --> 00:08:12.720
MARK MANDEL: So, Kubernetes,
it came from Google.

00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:14.720
I don't know that much
about the history itself,

00:08:14.720 --> 00:08:16.678
but I know, I believe,
Red Hat's been involved,

00:08:16.678 --> 00:08:19.110
and a few other companies.

00:08:19.110 --> 00:08:21.960
Do you know more about
where it was conceived

00:08:21.960 --> 00:08:23.790
and that sort of
background stuff?

00:08:23.790 --> 00:08:24.831
BRIAN DORSEY: Absolutely.

00:08:24.831 --> 00:08:29.700
So, basically, three very
senior folks at Google

00:08:29.700 --> 00:08:33.860
just noticed this kind
of trend of the rest

00:08:33.860 --> 00:08:37.289
of the world running into
these same situations

00:08:37.289 --> 00:08:41.760
that Google had a way of
dealing with internally,

00:08:41.760 --> 00:08:45.380
and decided to try
to prototype up,

00:08:45.380 --> 00:08:50.500
building an unusual version of
that that everybody could use.

00:08:50.500 --> 00:08:56.340
And then, we actually
convinced the company

00:08:56.340 --> 00:08:58.230
to release that as an
open source project,

00:08:58.230 --> 00:09:02.860
so that it runs entirely
on our Cloud platform.

00:09:02.860 --> 00:09:07.170
And now it is AWS and Azure,
and your own hardware.

00:09:07.170 --> 00:09:09.560
A lot of other places,
but-- it's very much

00:09:09.560 --> 00:09:13.565
taking the philosophies of
how Google runs software

00:09:13.565 --> 00:09:15.440
and releasing that as
an open source project.

00:09:15.440 --> 00:09:18.450
And so it started with a
few engineers at Google,

00:09:18.450 --> 00:09:21.340
and now, as you said,
Red Hat's involved,

00:09:21.340 --> 00:09:24.680
CoreOS is involved--
dozens, actually,

00:09:24.680 --> 00:09:27.160
of companies and
far more individuals

00:09:27.160 --> 00:09:29.140
from lots of other places.

00:09:29.140 --> 00:09:33.800
Actually, let's just take a look
real quick at the GitHub repo,

00:09:33.800 --> 00:09:44.180
which now has 10,000 stars,
2,700 forks, 19,700 commits as

00:09:44.180 --> 00:09:44.680
of today.

00:09:44.680 --> 00:09:45.360
MARK MANDEL: There are
a few people there,

00:09:45.360 --> 00:09:46.250
just a few people.

00:09:46.250 --> 00:09:46.590
BRIAN DORSEY: Yeah.

00:09:46.590 --> 00:09:47.965
MARK MANDEL: Just
a couple, here.

00:09:47.965 --> 00:09:50.740
BRIAN DORSEY: It's an extremely
active open source project,

00:09:50.740 --> 00:09:54.650
and we hit our 1.0 release
earlier this summer

00:09:54.650 --> 00:09:58.634
and heading very close
to the 1.1 release now.

00:09:58.634 --> 00:09:59.550
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:09:59.550 --> 00:10:04.380
So from a more
down-to-earth point of view,

00:10:04.380 --> 00:10:05.800
Kubernetes sounds pretty cool.

00:10:05.800 --> 00:10:08.630
It allows you to run
stuff on machines.

00:10:08.630 --> 00:10:11.910
I know that the stuff
itself-- it's basically

00:10:11.910 --> 00:10:12.952
Docker containers, right?

00:10:12.952 --> 00:10:13.785
BRIAN DORSEY: Right.

00:10:13.785 --> 00:10:15.640
That's the primary
container format.

00:10:15.640 --> 00:10:17.884
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Primary,
like, what other things

00:10:17.884 --> 00:10:18.800
can you run, actually?

00:10:18.800 --> 00:10:20.133
BRIAN DORSEY: Actually, so yeah.

00:10:20.133 --> 00:10:24.340
So the Rocket
container format that's

00:10:24.340 --> 00:10:27.960
from CoreOS is
supported, and then

00:10:27.960 --> 00:10:30.370
they've been moving
towards consolidating

00:10:30.370 --> 00:10:32.700
on-- so both Docker and
Rocket, and other folks,

00:10:32.700 --> 00:10:36.100
are moving to standardizing
some container formats,

00:10:36.100 --> 00:10:41.200
so-- Kubernetes is intended to
run multiple container formats,

00:10:41.200 --> 00:10:43.420
but definitely, primarily,
what people are running

00:10:43.420 --> 00:10:45.210
is Docker containers.

00:10:45.210 --> 00:10:46.160
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:10:46.160 --> 00:10:46.780
So OK.

00:10:46.780 --> 00:10:49.740
So then we're running
containers, mainly Docker,

00:10:49.740 --> 00:10:53.030
but not necessarily,
and Kubernetes

00:10:53.030 --> 00:10:56.280
allows us to just manage them.

00:10:56.280 --> 00:10:57.710
What does that even mean?

00:10:57.710 --> 00:10:59.100
What do you do with Kubernetes
that's your day-to-day thing?

00:10:59.100 --> 00:10:59.580
BRIAN DORSEY:
Right, and what does

00:10:59.580 --> 00:11:02.320
that look like from a developer
and operations perspective,

00:11:02.320 --> 00:11:02.820
right?

00:11:02.820 --> 00:11:03.120
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:11:03.120 --> 00:11:03.820
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:11:03.820 --> 00:11:05.590
BRIAN DORSEY: So the
container gives us

00:11:05.590 --> 00:11:09.940
a kind of hermetically sealed
container-- a little box

00:11:09.940 --> 00:11:10.940
for our processes.

00:11:10.940 --> 00:11:14.544
So just starting
there, it is amazing

00:11:14.544 --> 00:11:16.710
because you have a process
that every time it starts

00:11:16.710 --> 00:11:18.001
it has the same context, right?

00:11:18.001 --> 00:11:19.730
It always sees the
same file system,

00:11:19.730 --> 00:11:21.910
and that gets us all
kinds of great benefits.

00:11:21.910 --> 00:11:25.550
And one of those is you
can run that same package

00:11:25.550 --> 00:11:27.900
on different computers and
get the same result, right?

00:11:27.900 --> 00:11:32.680
Which is a huge pain, normally.

00:11:32.680 --> 00:11:34.810
And then what Kubernetes
really brings in

00:11:34.810 --> 00:11:38.050
is it keeps track
of all these things

00:11:38.050 --> 00:11:40.200
and it helps them
find each other.

00:11:40.200 --> 00:11:42.830
And, at a very
high level, it lets

00:11:42.830 --> 00:11:45.610
you talk about your whole
cluster declaratively.

00:11:45.610 --> 00:11:48.000
So when you're actually
using Kubernetes,

00:11:48.000 --> 00:11:52.040
you write a config, usually
in YAML, can also be JSON.

00:11:52.040 --> 00:11:54.600
And you say, OK, I've
got this container that

00:11:54.600 --> 00:11:57.090
has the version of the
software I want to run.

00:11:57.090 --> 00:12:01.600
I would like you to run five or
eight copies of this somewhere

00:12:01.600 --> 00:12:03.370
in the cluster.

00:12:03.370 --> 00:12:08.500
And I want to make sure that
they stay running, and go.

00:12:08.500 --> 00:12:11.210
And then you push that
declaration off to Kubenetes.

00:12:11.210 --> 00:12:13.540
And then, from then on,
it's Kubernetes' job

00:12:13.540 --> 00:12:17.970
to make sure that that
requested state stays true.

00:12:17.970 --> 00:12:20.300
So if one of those containers
dies, it will restart it.

00:12:20.300 --> 00:12:21.840
If one of the
hosts goes away, it

00:12:21.840 --> 00:12:26.150
will actually start up
new ones somewhere else.

00:12:26.150 --> 00:12:30.940
And so that lets you, in a
very real sense, sleep more.

00:12:30.940 --> 00:12:34.150
Because the system
is maintaining

00:12:34.150 --> 00:12:37.480
the way you asked
it to be set up,

00:12:37.480 --> 00:12:40.070
instead of just a one-time,
hey, run these things,

00:12:40.070 --> 00:12:43.060
and then you have to manually
somehow figure out how to,

00:12:43.060 --> 00:12:47.400
or, through automation, make
sure that they're running.

00:12:47.400 --> 00:12:50.262
MARK MANDEL: I think that--
two things you said that really

00:12:50.262 --> 00:12:52.845
resonate with me, and something
I really like about Kubernetes

00:12:52.845 --> 00:12:55.600
and its ecosystems,
one of which is you've

00:12:55.600 --> 00:12:57.490
got this Docker format
that's ubiquitous,

00:12:57.490 --> 00:12:59.242
and I think that's
incredibly powerful.

00:12:59.242 --> 00:13:00.700
And when you said
earlier, you were

00:13:00.700 --> 00:13:03.600
talking about coming
from Borg to Kubernetes--

00:13:03.600 --> 00:13:05.500
they're able to look at
it and say, OK, these

00:13:05.500 --> 00:13:08.030
are common things that people
are doing in terms of scale,

00:13:08.030 --> 00:13:10.607
in terms of managing computers,
clusters, stuff like that,

00:13:10.607 --> 00:13:12.190
and putting those
two things together.

00:13:12.190 --> 00:13:14.616
It doesn't matter what that
application inside that Docker

00:13:14.616 --> 00:13:15.490
container looks like.

00:13:15.490 --> 00:13:19.040
You can be as unique and
individual a snowflake as you

00:13:19.040 --> 00:13:21.510
make inside your own
little workspace.

00:13:21.510 --> 00:13:23.757
But then once you wrap
that up, you can just

00:13:23.757 --> 00:13:25.340
become this ubiquitous
format that you

00:13:25.340 --> 00:13:28.452
can do standard things with,
and you're no longer going,

00:13:28.452 --> 00:13:29.910
I need a scaling
solution for Ruby.

00:13:29.910 --> 00:13:31.750
Or I need a scaling
solution for Node.

00:13:31.750 --> 00:13:33.990
Or I need a scaling solution
for my particular app

00:13:33.990 --> 00:13:35.819
that's very unique and special.

00:13:35.819 --> 00:13:38.360
You can take advantage of these
pretty awesome tools, which I

00:13:38.360 --> 00:13:40.040
think is super, super powerful.

00:13:40.040 --> 00:13:40.956
BRIAN DORSEY: Exactly.

00:13:40.956 --> 00:13:42.880
And if your dev
workflow-- so you've got

00:13:42.880 --> 00:13:44.890
developers working,
and then you've

00:13:44.890 --> 00:13:47.452
got testers actually
testing, and making sure

00:13:47.452 --> 00:13:48.160
that things work.

00:13:48.160 --> 00:13:51.470
Maybe the same folks and
then operations folks.

00:13:51.470 --> 00:13:53.310
No matter which
pieces of software

00:13:53.310 --> 00:13:55.726
the different containers use,
you can use the same process

00:13:55.726 --> 00:13:57.860
across all of those.

00:13:57.860 --> 00:14:00.820
And really, we all
want to choose the best

00:14:00.820 --> 00:14:04.152
tool for the job or different
teams in a real service org,

00:14:04.152 --> 00:14:06.610
your network structure might
choose to use different tools.

00:14:06.610 --> 00:14:09.880
And this lets you
manage them all.

00:14:09.880 --> 00:14:11.720
That's one of the
fundamental benefits

00:14:11.720 --> 00:14:14.340
of Docker and containerization.

00:14:14.340 --> 00:14:16.340
FRANCESC CAMPOY: One of
the things that I really

00:14:16.340 --> 00:14:20.370
like about Kubernetes is the
fact that what you were saying,

00:14:20.370 --> 00:14:23.470
this concept of desired
state, where basically,

00:14:23.470 --> 00:14:26.252
rather than having
to you handles,

00:14:26.252 --> 00:14:27.460
oh, these machines went down.

00:14:27.460 --> 00:14:30.970
Let me go, and we start this
job somewhere else that's

00:14:30.970 --> 00:14:32.910
going to be automated directly.

00:14:32.910 --> 00:14:37.039
And that's cool, but something
which, I think, is even cooler,

00:14:37.039 --> 00:14:38.580
and I've seen some
demos of it, but I

00:14:38.580 --> 00:14:40.788
think you could explain a
little bit more what it is.

00:14:40.788 --> 00:14:43.274
It's this concept
of rolling updates.

00:14:43.274 --> 00:14:44.440
BRIAN DORSEY: Oh absolutely.

00:14:44.440 --> 00:14:44.810
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Because I've seen

00:14:44.810 --> 00:14:47.020
that-- I've seen that at
Google, but I have never

00:14:47.020 --> 00:14:48.700
seen that outside of Google.

00:14:48.700 --> 00:14:50.714
BRIAN DORSEY: It's
super powerful.

00:14:50.714 --> 00:14:52.130
So we've got desired
state, right?

00:14:52.130 --> 00:14:54.630
And when we first set
up the thing I told it,

00:14:54.630 --> 00:14:57.410
I want five web frontends.

00:14:57.410 --> 00:15:00.570
But that number-- you can
change that over time.

00:15:00.570 --> 00:15:03.030
So you can think of it as,
there's a dial, like a volume

00:15:03.030 --> 00:15:04.640
knob, on your processes.

00:15:04.640 --> 00:15:07.560
You can-- let's
turn that up to 11.

00:15:07.560 --> 00:15:10.560
And you get all the
processes running, right?

00:15:10.560 --> 00:15:12.404
And so you can turn
it up and down there,

00:15:12.404 --> 00:15:14.320
so that just gets you
your scaling right away.

00:15:14.320 --> 00:15:16.403
So you just tell Kubernetes,
oh, I want eight now,

00:15:16.403 --> 00:15:17.980
I want three now.

00:15:17.980 --> 00:15:19.800
And then the rolling
update-- so as you

00:15:19.800 --> 00:15:21.550
have new versions
of your software,

00:15:21.550 --> 00:15:25.420
you can basically
say, OK, I'm going

00:15:25.420 --> 00:15:28.330
to create a new definition
for this new version,

00:15:28.330 --> 00:15:31.450
and Kubernetes, I want
you to slowly start

00:15:31.450 --> 00:15:34.340
adding more of these,
and I'll watch the logs.

00:15:34.340 --> 00:15:36.090
And as long as
everything's going well,

00:15:36.090 --> 00:15:39.360
we'll just slowly replace the
old ones with the new ones.

00:15:39.360 --> 00:15:41.400
And then we'll have a new
version in production.

00:15:41.400 --> 00:15:43.940
And so that's pretty powerful.

00:15:43.940 --> 00:15:46.270
But since these containers
are hermetically sealed,

00:15:46.270 --> 00:15:49.710
I think that they're isolated.

00:15:49.710 --> 00:15:51.920
They're this one
thing so you can

00:15:51.920 --> 00:15:53.860
do this atomic
upgrade, where you just

00:15:53.860 --> 00:15:55.800
replace the previous
version with a new one.

00:15:55.800 --> 00:15:58.160
But the crazy thing is,
a rollback is actually

00:15:58.160 --> 00:16:00.665
the exact same process.

00:16:00.665 --> 00:16:01.290
So going to a--

00:16:01.290 --> 00:16:01.740
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
That's really nice.

00:16:01.740 --> 00:16:03.910
BRIAN DORSEY: Previous
version is the same, right?

00:16:03.910 --> 00:16:06.069
And that blows my mind.

00:16:06.069 --> 00:16:07.110
MARK MANDEL: I love this.

00:16:07.110 --> 00:16:10.570
Especially as someone-- I've
hand-rolled stuff behind a load

00:16:10.570 --> 00:16:12.836
balancer, just because
I haven't either

00:16:12.836 --> 00:16:14.210
had the time, or
the inclination,

00:16:14.210 --> 00:16:16.790
or the pain point to actually
go in and automate this stuff.

00:16:16.790 --> 00:16:18.430
So being able to
just go, oh, well

00:16:18.430 --> 00:16:21.050
this thing gives me a way to
rolling update behind a load

00:16:21.050 --> 00:16:22.730
balancer or whatever
it's in front of,

00:16:22.730 --> 00:16:24.290
that's super powerful.

00:16:24.290 --> 00:16:26.844
Again, a generic solution
for a problem that we've all

00:16:26.844 --> 00:16:28.260
encountered in one
way or another.

00:16:28.260 --> 00:16:30.509
BRIAN DORSEY: So let's dive
in generic solution a bit.

00:16:30.509 --> 00:16:33.030
So we've talked about
running these processes.

00:16:33.030 --> 00:16:36.650
So that's basically a system
D or a process manager

00:16:36.650 --> 00:16:39.240
kind of thing, but across
a whole bunch of machines.

00:16:39.240 --> 00:16:41.942
But you also have to figure
out where these things are.

00:16:41.942 --> 00:16:44.150
So if you want to connect
them up to a load balancer,

00:16:44.150 --> 00:16:47.422
like you mentioned, you have
to do that connection somehow.

00:16:47.422 --> 00:16:48.880
You have to tell
the load balancer.

00:16:48.880 --> 00:16:52.004
So Kubernetes
handles that as well.

00:16:52.004 --> 00:16:53.670
So as you make this
definition, you also

00:16:53.670 --> 00:16:55.590
make this definition
just called a service,

00:16:55.590 --> 00:16:57.635
and that-- Kubernetes
is aware of where

00:16:57.635 --> 00:16:59.510
these things are actually
running and handles

00:16:59.510 --> 00:17:02.680
the plumbing for connecting
a load balancer to it.

00:17:02.680 --> 00:17:06.940
And that also gives you this
nice static name, which is also

00:17:06.940 --> 00:17:08.680
a DNS name and IP address.

00:17:08.680 --> 00:17:12.450
So you can have each of the
different parts of your system

00:17:12.450 --> 00:17:15.079
have a static name,
but the actual--

00:17:15.079 --> 00:17:19.094
where those things exist--
that changes over time.

00:17:19.094 --> 00:17:20.260
So it might be running here.

00:17:20.260 --> 00:17:21.579
It might be running
there, and your software

00:17:21.579 --> 00:17:22.453
doesn't have to care.

00:17:22.453 --> 00:17:23.920
You've got a known
location to talk

00:17:23.920 --> 00:17:26.169
to your cache, a known
location talk to your database,

00:17:26.169 --> 00:17:28.880
a known location to
talk to web frontends.

00:17:28.880 --> 00:17:31.400
And Kubernetes just kind
of handles all that.

00:17:31.400 --> 00:17:33.750
And then you can plumb
logging through and all kinds

00:17:33.750 --> 00:17:34.809
of other fun stuff.

00:17:34.809 --> 00:17:36.850
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah I
guess that's a difference

00:17:36.850 --> 00:17:41.440
between schedulers
and Kubernetes.

00:17:41.440 --> 00:17:44.340
Kubernetes is not just
scheduling containers.

00:17:44.340 --> 00:17:45.490
BRIAN DORSEY: Absolutely.

00:17:45.490 --> 00:17:48.310
FRANCESC CAMPOY: It's actually
a little bit more than that

00:17:48.310 --> 00:17:52.450
because there's service--
service is basically

00:17:52.450 --> 00:17:53.720
service discovery.

00:17:53.720 --> 00:17:53.950
BRIAN DORSEY: Yes.

00:17:53.950 --> 00:17:56.533
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So you get that
already inside of Kubernetes.

00:17:56.533 --> 00:17:58.552
You don't need to add
another layer on top.

00:17:58.552 --> 00:18:00.010
MARK MANDEL: So to
take a step back

00:18:00.010 --> 00:18:02.010
for those not familiar
with the term scheduling,

00:18:02.010 --> 00:18:03.802
Do you want to
explain what that is?

00:18:03.802 --> 00:18:05.760
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So,
yeah, basically scheduling

00:18:05.760 --> 00:18:11.340
could be I want to run
five copies of this,

00:18:11.340 --> 00:18:13.490
and then just run them.

00:18:13.490 --> 00:18:15.310
If one of the
machines goes down,

00:18:15.310 --> 00:18:17.570
then just move that
container somewhere else.

00:18:17.570 --> 00:18:20.794
But then I want to be
able to say, oh yeah,

00:18:20.794 --> 00:18:21.960
I'm going to send a request.

00:18:21.960 --> 00:18:24.820
Those requests should be handled
by one of those five containers

00:18:24.820 --> 00:18:25.520
that are running

00:18:25.520 --> 00:18:26.270
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:18:26.270 --> 00:18:28.270
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Well,
if they keep them moving,

00:18:28.270 --> 00:18:29.370
how do I target them?

00:18:29.370 --> 00:18:32.030
So a service is basically
just a way of saying, oh,

00:18:32.030 --> 00:18:35.640
just send it to this
service, and Kubernetes just

00:18:35.640 --> 00:18:37.854
will find whoever is
behind that service.

00:18:37.854 --> 00:18:39.270
MARK MANDEL: So
they intelligently

00:18:39.270 --> 00:18:40.520
route through whatever--

00:18:40.520 --> 00:18:42.145
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And
what I really like

00:18:42.145 --> 00:18:43.820
is the fact that the
way you define it

00:18:43.820 --> 00:18:47.200
is via a very simple
concept, which is tags,

00:18:47.200 --> 00:18:48.220
and that's so beautiful.

00:18:48.220 --> 00:18:51.650
It's basically just tag stuff
saying, this is the backend,

00:18:51.650 --> 00:18:54.660
or this is the frontend, or
this is something for test.

00:18:54.660 --> 00:18:56.550
And then you can
create service saying,

00:18:56.550 --> 00:18:59.330
oh yeah, target all the
frontends that are also test.

00:18:59.330 --> 00:19:01.657
So things like that,
which is really simple,

00:19:01.657 --> 00:19:03.240
but at the same time,
it's so powerful

00:19:03.240 --> 00:19:05.656
that [INAUDIBLE] has to use.

00:19:05.656 --> 00:19:07.072
BRIAN DORSEY: And,
yeah, those are

00:19:07.072 --> 00:19:08.821
a key part of the
rolling update and helps

00:19:08.821 --> 00:19:14.290
you do-- people who do red/black
upgrade and that sort of thing.

00:19:14.290 --> 00:19:17.240
You also mentioned the
other part of scheduling

00:19:17.240 --> 00:19:21.141
is making sure that you
put [INAUDIBLE] processes

00:19:21.141 --> 00:19:23.140
in a place where they're
going to be successful.

00:19:23.140 --> 00:19:24.639
You put them on a
machine that's not

00:19:24.639 --> 00:19:28.370
too busy, that has enough
memory, and that works.

00:19:28.370 --> 00:19:29.830
Mandy Waite on
our team describes

00:19:29.830 --> 00:19:35.380
that as-- you're kind of playing
Tetris with your resources.

00:19:35.380 --> 00:19:39.020
You've got these oddly-shaped
combinations of CPU and memory,

00:19:39.020 --> 00:19:40.840
and disk requirements,
and then they

00:19:40.840 --> 00:19:42.320
need to fit in
these certain size

00:19:42.320 --> 00:19:45.390
boxes that are the actual nodes
you're running the software on.

00:19:45.390 --> 00:19:47.510
And you want to maximize
that because that's

00:19:47.510 --> 00:19:48.530
the most efficient.

00:19:48.530 --> 00:19:50.840
It's going to save
you the most money.

00:19:50.840 --> 00:19:55.060
And it quickly becomes this kind
of multi-dimensional complex

00:19:55.060 --> 00:19:57.240
game of Tetris and--

00:19:57.240 --> 00:19:59.976
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I really like
that comparison with Tetris.

00:19:59.976 --> 00:20:02.480
I wonder if you've seen--
Kelsey Hightower gave

00:20:02.480 --> 00:20:08.640
a really good talk, talking
about Kubernetes at HashiConf.

00:20:08.640 --> 00:20:11.034
And he actually played
Tetris in front of everybody

00:20:11.034 --> 00:20:11.700
during his talk.

00:20:11.700 --> 00:20:12.240
BRIAN DORSEY: Oh,
that's fantastic.

00:20:12.240 --> 00:20:12.590
MARK MANDEL: That's fantastic.

00:20:12.590 --> 00:20:13.940
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That
was really good, yeah.

00:20:13.940 --> 00:20:14.660
BRIAN DORSEY: That
is super fantastic.

00:20:14.660 --> 00:20:16.701
FRANCESC CAMPOY: To bring
home the same point of,

00:20:16.701 --> 00:20:19.230
yeah, it's hard to do,
but it's automated.

00:20:19.230 --> 00:20:22.000
MARK MANDEL: Well, I think we
actually had a Cloud Spin win,

00:20:22.000 --> 00:20:25.540
so if people have ever
seen our Cloud Spin demo,

00:20:25.540 --> 00:20:28.391
we were running it on virtual
machines for a long time

00:20:28.391 --> 00:20:30.890
to do our video processing, and
we shifted it to Kubernetes.

00:20:30.890 --> 00:20:34.279
And I think we got something
like a three times less

00:20:34.279 --> 00:20:36.070
resource usage or
something to that effect.

00:20:36.070 --> 00:20:36.986
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:20:36.986 --> 00:20:39.910
Yeah, I mean, when I created the
demo, basically, it was, like,

00:20:39.910 --> 00:20:41.720
I just need to get it working.

00:20:41.720 --> 00:20:44.700
So I did something that
I'm sure most of us

00:20:44.700 --> 00:20:47.400
have done before,
which is I'm running

00:20:47.400 --> 00:20:48.720
one container per machine.

00:20:48.720 --> 00:20:49.180
MARK MANDEL: Yup.

00:20:49.180 --> 00:20:49.430
BRIAN DORSEY: Yup.

00:20:49.430 --> 00:20:50.500
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And if
the machine goes down,

00:20:50.500 --> 00:20:51.210
just restart it.

00:20:51.210 --> 00:20:52.065
That's it.

00:20:52.065 --> 00:20:53.362
So that's easy.

00:20:53.362 --> 00:20:55.070
But yeah, we're
spending-- we're spending

00:20:55.070 --> 00:20:57.990
a serious amount of
money because you're

00:20:57.990 --> 00:21:00.345
using one machine per
container, which is not really

00:21:00.345 --> 00:21:01.390
what you want to do.

00:21:01.390 --> 00:21:03.620
And with Kubernetes,
basically, we

00:21:03.620 --> 00:21:07.080
ended up with just three hosts
and all these containers,

00:21:07.080 --> 00:21:09.480
same amount of containers,
so same performance

00:21:09.480 --> 00:21:11.770
that we used to
have, but automated

00:21:11.770 --> 00:21:12.930
over only three instances.

00:21:12.930 --> 00:21:13.680
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:21:13.680 --> 00:21:15.221
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
And that was really,

00:21:15.221 --> 00:21:18.490
really nice and really
easy to do so, yeah.

00:21:18.490 --> 00:21:19.860
MARK MANDEL: Very, very cool.

00:21:19.860 --> 00:21:23.430
OK, so I guess we
should probably

00:21:23.430 --> 00:21:25.362
start talking about
Google Container Engine.

00:21:25.362 --> 00:21:26.320
BRIAN DORSEY: Oh right.

00:21:26.320 --> 00:21:27.230
Google Cloud podcast.

00:21:27.230 --> 00:21:29.271
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
Google Container with a K.

00:21:29.271 --> 00:21:31.885
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, it is a
Google Cloud Platform podcast.

00:21:31.885 --> 00:21:32.170
We probably should talk
about those products.

00:21:32.170 --> 00:21:34.503
BRIAN DORSEY: Yeah, actually,
we should talk about that.

00:21:34.503 --> 00:21:39.742
So people sometimes abbreviate
Google Container Engine as GKE.

00:21:39.742 --> 00:21:40.450
MARK MANDEL: Yes.

00:21:40.450 --> 00:21:41.949
BRIAN DORSEY: So
you can think of it

00:21:41.949 --> 00:21:44.650
as Google Kubernetes Engine.

00:21:44.650 --> 00:21:45.390
MARK MANDEL: Yup.

00:21:45.390 --> 00:21:47.830
BRIAN DORSEY: In a way.

00:21:47.830 --> 00:21:50.830
And so the main
difference there is

00:21:50.830 --> 00:21:54.940
it's running-- Container Engine
is hosted Kubernetes, so it's

00:21:54.940 --> 00:21:57.780
running the open
source Kubernetes,

00:21:57.780 --> 00:22:00.300
but we handle running
the master for you.

00:22:00.300 --> 00:22:04.000
So we maintain that,
we keep it up-to-date,

00:22:04.000 --> 00:22:09.370
we upgrade the Kubernetes
software itself over time.

00:22:09.370 --> 00:22:11.240
We just kind of
basically handle--

00:22:11.240 --> 00:22:14.920
you can think about actually
using Kubernetes in two parts.

00:22:14.920 --> 00:22:18.380
There's one part that is
setting up the cluster itself

00:22:18.380 --> 00:22:20.880
and running Kubernetes,
and then the other part

00:22:20.880 --> 00:22:22.730
is like running your
software in Kubernetes.

00:22:22.730 --> 00:22:24.354
So you're kind of
like-- the config

00:22:24.354 --> 00:22:26.020
and setup and maintenance
of the cluster

00:22:26.020 --> 00:22:28.910
itself versus actually
using the cluster.

00:22:28.910 --> 00:22:30.410
And actually,
internally, at Google,

00:22:30.410 --> 00:22:33.150
it's two separate groups who
usually manage those things.

00:22:33.150 --> 00:22:34.730
You've got the people
that run all the clusters,

00:22:34.730 --> 00:22:35.810
and then you've
got the application

00:22:35.810 --> 00:22:38.101
groups who actually run their
software in the clusters.

00:22:40.240 --> 00:22:43.290
So this is a way of,
basically, letting Google

00:22:43.290 --> 00:22:46.884
run Kubernetes for you so
you can focus on your app.

00:22:46.884 --> 00:22:48.300
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
So, you mentioned

00:22:48.300 --> 00:22:52.340
that one of the things that
Google Container Engine does

00:22:52.340 --> 00:22:54.150
is create a cluster.

00:22:54.150 --> 00:22:58.560
If you were to create your
own cluster, what do you do?

00:22:58.560 --> 00:23:03.880
You get, I have, I don't know,
five machines in my premises.

00:23:03.880 --> 00:23:05.130
BRIAN DORSEY: Right.

00:23:05.130 --> 00:23:07.630
FRANCESC CAMPOY: How hard
is it to get it working?

00:23:07.630 --> 00:23:10.770
BRIAN DORSEY: So it-- so there
are a couple things you get.

00:23:10.770 --> 00:23:14.990
So Kubernetes comes out of
the cloud kind of world.

00:23:14.990 --> 00:23:17.680
And so there's some stuff you
get in those environments,

00:23:17.680 --> 00:23:19.760
so whether that's
on Google Cloud

00:23:19.760 --> 00:23:25.250
or on other folks' clouds,
you get the dynamic creation

00:23:25.250 --> 00:23:26.710
and removal of machines.

00:23:26.710 --> 00:23:29.180
You get APIs for
controlling networks.

00:23:29.180 --> 00:23:33.560
And those are actually
the two main benefits

00:23:33.560 --> 00:23:35.980
that are a little more--
that are contrasting

00:23:35.980 --> 00:23:37.190
to running on your machines.

00:23:37.190 --> 00:23:38.606
So the network,
in particular, can

00:23:38.606 --> 00:23:40.910
be a little bit of a challenge
right now when you're

00:23:40.910 --> 00:23:42.445
setting up your own stuff.

00:23:42.445 --> 00:23:43.820
One of the things
Kubernetes does

00:23:43.820 --> 00:23:48.550
is it gives every pod-- every
collection of containers

00:23:48.550 --> 00:23:49.665
has its own IP address.

00:23:49.665 --> 00:23:51.790
And so that's really helped
simplify the scheduling

00:23:51.790 --> 00:23:53.660
because you don't have to
ever deal with port conflicts,

00:23:53.660 --> 00:23:54.160
or whatever.

00:23:54.160 --> 00:23:56.460
If two different things
are listening on port 80,

00:23:56.460 --> 00:23:57.834
they're trying to
be web servers,

00:23:57.834 --> 00:24:00.500
You can still schedule
them on the same machine.

00:24:00.500 --> 00:24:02.417
They get their own IP address.

00:24:02.417 --> 00:24:04.500
In turn, though, that means
that Kubernetes itself

00:24:04.500 --> 00:24:06.890
needs to have some
control over the network.

00:24:06.890 --> 00:24:12.110
That's not super common
in people's physical data

00:24:12.110 --> 00:24:13.400
centers a lot of times.

00:24:13.400 --> 00:24:16.010
So people often run
an overlay network,

00:24:16.010 --> 00:24:20.490
like Flannel, or
change the way the load

00:24:20.490 --> 00:24:23.060
balancing in the networking
works a little bit to do that.

00:24:23.060 --> 00:24:26.460
So that's usually probably
the main challenge.

00:24:26.460 --> 00:24:28.377
Other than that,
it's a Go program,

00:24:28.377 --> 00:24:30.210
and we've got setup
guides for a whole bunch

00:24:30.210 --> 00:24:32.795
of different environments
up on Kubernetes.io.

00:24:35.846 --> 00:24:38.220
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And so there's
some guys and everything,

00:24:38.220 --> 00:24:39.750
you have to install
some software

00:24:39.750 --> 00:24:42.590
and make sure that the
machines are well-connected,

00:24:42.590 --> 00:24:46.850
and all the networking stuff,
which is really hard for me

00:24:46.850 --> 00:24:47.350
to do.

00:24:47.350 --> 00:24:49.200
I'm not a networking guy.

00:24:49.200 --> 00:24:51.930
I have to set that up.

00:24:51.930 --> 00:24:55.660
With GKE-- Google
Container Engine, how long

00:24:55.660 --> 00:24:57.660
does it take to
create a cluster?

00:24:57.660 --> 00:24:59.580
BRIAN DORSEY:
About five minutes.

00:24:59.580 --> 00:25:02.097
And most of that's just
machines starting up,

00:25:02.097 --> 00:25:03.930
and scripts running,
and that sort of thing.

00:25:03.930 --> 00:25:06.270
So it's about a
minute of your time,

00:25:06.270 --> 00:25:08.470
so you log into
the Cloud Console

00:25:08.470 --> 00:25:10.440
and go over to--
navigate your way down

00:25:10.440 --> 00:25:13.520
the sidebar to
containers, and give it

00:25:13.520 --> 00:25:16.140
a name, how many nodes
you want to have in it,

00:25:16.140 --> 00:25:18.520
what size computers
those nodes are,

00:25:18.520 --> 00:25:21.867
how many processors
they have, and hit go.

00:25:21.867 --> 00:25:24.200
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And you're
ready to run your containers

00:25:24.200 --> 00:25:24.730
in there.

00:25:24.730 --> 00:25:25.520
That's nice.

00:25:25.520 --> 00:25:26.000
BRIAN DORSEY: Yeah, it's--

00:25:26.000 --> 00:25:27.666
MARK MANDEL: It's the
only way I've ever

00:25:27.666 --> 00:25:29.170
fired up a Kubernetes cluster.

00:25:29.170 --> 00:25:30.909
I've never tried to
manually do it yet.

00:25:30.909 --> 00:25:31.700
I'm not that brave.

00:25:31.700 --> 00:25:33.440
BRIAN DORSEY: On
that note, no matter

00:25:33.440 --> 00:25:35.740
where you're planning on
running Kubernetes long-term,

00:25:35.740 --> 00:25:38.510
and I think one of the big
advantages of Kubernetes

00:25:38.510 --> 00:25:41.886
is that it gives you some
consistency to environment

00:25:41.886 --> 00:25:43.260
for running your
software, so you

00:25:43.260 --> 00:25:45.370
can run it in multiple
different cloud providers.

00:25:45.370 --> 00:25:46.869
You can run it on
your own machines.

00:25:46.869 --> 00:25:48.700
You can run it on your laptop.

00:25:48.700 --> 00:25:53.060
You can run the same system
in a lot of different places.

00:25:53.060 --> 00:25:55.720
But if you're trying to
decide whether or not

00:25:55.720 --> 00:25:58.700
this is something you even
want to spend the time on,

00:25:58.700 --> 00:26:01.802
doing your first experiments
in Google Container Engine

00:26:01.802 --> 00:26:03.760
is just so much easier
to tell whether you even

00:26:03.760 --> 00:26:05.510
care about Kubernetes--
whether you

00:26:05.510 --> 00:26:08.868
want to spend the time to
set it up on your own stuff.

00:26:08.868 --> 00:26:09.820
MARK MANDEL: Yup.

00:26:09.820 --> 00:26:11.611
BRIAN DORSEY: So I'd
highly recommend that.

00:26:11.611 --> 00:26:13.940
No matter where you
plan on deploying.

00:26:13.940 --> 00:26:15.802
MARK MANDEL: Yeah I agree 100%.

00:26:15.802 --> 00:26:17.510
I've seen more than
a few people do that.

00:26:17.510 --> 00:26:19.600
And yet, one of the lovely
things about Kubernetes

00:26:19.600 --> 00:26:23.030
is that you can pick it up
and move it, which is great.

00:26:23.030 --> 00:26:25.960
So, wow, we would love for
you to stay on Google Cloud

00:26:25.960 --> 00:26:26.540
Platform.

00:26:26.540 --> 00:26:28.750
We understand that
people have needs.

00:26:28.750 --> 00:26:31.410
That's fair enough.

00:26:31.410 --> 00:26:33.990
But yeah, it's just
such an easy process

00:26:33.990 --> 00:26:37.750
to get three or four nodes spun
up and start applying things,

00:26:37.750 --> 00:26:38.750
and get playing with it.

00:26:38.750 --> 00:26:39.790
It's great.

00:26:39.790 --> 00:26:42.331
