things like that.

00:29:36.450 --> 00:29:38.810
A couple things I wanted
to call out in particular

00:29:38.810 --> 00:29:44.840
is there's a hook
that you can basically

00:29:44.840 --> 00:29:49.050
set up an HTTP listener or run
a script right before a pod is

00:29:49.050 --> 00:29:50.330
shut down.

00:29:50.330 --> 00:29:51.000
So sometimes--

00:29:51.000 --> 00:29:51.920
MARK MANDEL: Oh.

00:29:51.920 --> 00:29:52.836
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:29:52.836 --> 00:29:54.550
BRIAN DORSEY: Your
code-- Kubernetes

00:29:54.550 --> 00:29:57.400
decides that you need
to get off this host.

00:29:57.400 --> 00:30:00.280
And so if it's a
planned event, you'll

00:30:00.280 --> 00:30:02.280
get a hook so that you
can gracefully shut down.

00:30:02.280 --> 00:30:04.020
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So you can
save some stuff to disk,

00:30:04.020 --> 00:30:05.305
or whatever you need to do.

00:30:05.305 --> 00:30:05.750
MARK MANDEL: Clean
up, yeah, nice.

00:30:05.750 --> 00:30:06.550
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Yes, that's nice.

00:30:06.550 --> 00:30:08.130
BRIAN DORSEY: So
that's super nice.

00:30:08.130 --> 00:30:11.645
There's a new job object
coming, so this is going to be

00:30:11.645 --> 00:30:14.090
the building blocks for
more batch operations. .

00:30:14.090 --> 00:30:17.780
Right now, Kubernetes is focused
on long-running processes.

00:30:17.780 --> 00:30:21.320
The job object really
brings in-- you could always

00:30:21.320 --> 00:30:25.792
run a single container, a
single pod, to completion.

00:30:25.792 --> 00:30:27.750
Just say, I want to run
this and make it happy.

00:30:27.750 --> 00:30:29.850
And Kubernetes has always
been able to do that.

00:30:29.850 --> 00:30:35.210
But the job object allows you
to manage a large number of jobs

00:30:35.210 --> 00:30:38.060
that you might want to
only run a subset at a time

00:30:38.060 --> 00:30:39.980
to completion.

00:30:39.980 --> 00:30:42.330
So you can set up queue workers.

00:30:42.330 --> 00:30:45.550
You can set up--
if you want to run

00:30:45.550 --> 00:30:48.330
1,000 instances of
something, but you only

00:30:48.330 --> 00:30:51.444
have room to run 25
of them at a time,

00:30:51.444 --> 00:30:53.360
you can use this to
manage that sort of thing.

00:30:53.360 --> 00:30:54.690
MARK MANDEL: Can you
do cron-type things

00:30:54.690 --> 00:30:56.230
where you want to do
something once every hour,

00:30:56.230 --> 00:30:57.200
or something like that?

00:30:57.200 --> 00:31:01.774
BRIAN DORSEY: So, I don't-- I'm
not aware of a cron right now.

00:31:01.774 --> 00:31:02.440
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:31:02.440 --> 00:31:04.398
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That
sounds more like-- almost

00:31:04.398 --> 00:31:05.420
like MapReduce stuff.

00:31:05.420 --> 00:31:05.760
BRIAN DORSEY: Yeah

00:31:05.760 --> 00:31:07.140
FRANCESC CAMPOY: It's
a lot of things to do

00:31:07.140 --> 00:31:08.350
but not enough resources.

00:31:08.350 --> 00:31:08.420
MARK MANDEL: That works.

00:31:08.420 --> 00:31:08.900
BRIAN DORSEY: Absolutely.

00:31:08.900 --> 00:31:09.140
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK.

00:31:09.140 --> 00:31:10.410
BRIAN DORSEY: Yes so
you can definitely

00:31:10.410 --> 00:31:12.710
do MapReduce-y kinds of things,
a lot of data processing

00:31:12.710 --> 00:31:13.080
sort of things.

00:31:13.080 --> 00:31:13.435
It's definitely--

00:31:13.435 --> 00:31:13.690
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That's nice.

00:31:13.690 --> 00:31:13.950
MARK MANDEL: That works.

00:31:13.950 --> 00:31:14.200
That makes sense.

00:31:14.200 --> 00:31:16.140
BRIAN DORSEY: Hitting
those targets.

00:31:16.140 --> 00:31:18.640
Cron is super easy to implement
with Kubernetes, by the way,

00:31:18.640 --> 00:31:21.098
because you can basically take
your config of the thing you

00:31:21.098 --> 00:31:23.530
want to do, and you can
run kubectl, and just say,

00:31:23.530 --> 00:31:25.946
create this pod,
run to completion.

00:31:25.946 --> 00:31:26.696
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:31:26.696 --> 00:31:28.112
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Nice, nice, yeah.

00:31:28.112 --> 00:31:30.900
BRIAN DORSEY: And just put
that in a cron somewhere,

00:31:30.900 --> 00:31:32.771
and that'll do its thing.

00:31:32.771 --> 00:31:34.770
And then the other one I
really want to call out

00:31:34.770 --> 00:31:36.341
is autoscaling pods.

00:31:36.341 --> 00:31:36.840
So--

00:31:36.840 --> 00:31:38.965
MARK MANDEL: Ah, we get a
lot of requests for that.

00:31:38.965 --> 00:31:40.310
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yup.

00:31:40.310 --> 00:31:42.090
BRIAN DORSEY: So you
can basically say,

00:31:42.090 --> 00:31:45.270
I want to run no fewer
than this many and no more

00:31:45.270 --> 00:31:48.600
than this many, but,
within that range,

00:31:48.600 --> 00:31:52.197
run enough to keep memory or CPU
targets in this general area.

00:31:52.197 --> 00:31:53.280
And then it will kind of--

00:31:53.280 --> 00:31:54.863
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Ah,
that's very nice.

00:31:54.863 --> 00:31:56.472
BRIAN DORSEY: Up and down.

00:31:56.472 --> 00:31:57.930
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
That's really cool

