00:35:09.300 --> 00:35:14.010
come in from one of our
listeners, an Alex Van Boxel.

00:35:14.010 --> 00:35:18.300
He's asked us, we have a
lot of data in BigQuery,

00:35:18.300 --> 00:35:22.690
and he's looking to divide the
data into buckets per date.

00:35:22.690 --> 00:35:24.490
He's quite aware that
a table can never

00:35:24.490 --> 00:35:27.350
be too big because
BigQuery's awesome,

00:35:27.350 --> 00:35:30.030
but he's wondering whether
it can be too small.

00:35:30.030 --> 00:35:33.710
What is the size that is
too small for BigQuery?

00:35:33.710 --> 00:35:35.999
So I think this is a really
interesting question.

00:35:35.999 --> 00:35:38.040
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah
that's the kind of question

00:35:38.040 --> 00:35:39.998
I am very curious about
because I didn't really

00:35:39.998 --> 00:35:40.744
know the answer.

00:35:40.744 --> 00:35:42.160
MARK MANDEL: Yes,
so we had a chat

00:35:42.160 --> 00:35:44.670
with a few people
about this, and they

00:35:44.670 --> 00:35:46.370
had a few different
perspectives, which

00:35:46.370 --> 00:35:49.720
I thought were quite cool.

00:35:49.720 --> 00:35:53.930
We had one of our teammates,
Felipe, brought up

00:35:53.930 --> 00:35:56.680
a very interesting point
that he pointed out

00:35:56.680 --> 00:35:58.025
in the documentation.

00:35:58.025 --> 00:36:00.400
He said that charges are
rounded to the nearest megabyte,

00:36:00.400 --> 00:36:03.940
with a minimum of 10 megabytes
of data processed per table.

00:36:03.940 --> 00:36:05.690
So it's something
to keep in mind

00:36:05.690 --> 00:36:07.750
when doing stuff with BigQuery.

00:36:07.750 --> 00:36:10.157
If you have tables with
really small amounts of data,

00:36:10.157 --> 00:36:11.990
you're always going to
get charged a minimum

00:36:11.990 --> 00:36:13.814
of 10 megabytes per query.

00:36:13.814 --> 00:36:14.730
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:36:14.730 --> 00:36:16.990
Nowadays, no, I don't
think that anyone actually

00:36:16.990 --> 00:36:19.840
has a table with less than
10 megs, but if you do, yeah.

00:36:19.840 --> 00:36:20.930
It's good to keep in mind.

00:36:20.930 --> 00:36:23.054
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, well,
I think that could come up

00:36:23.054 --> 00:36:24.660
if people leave
their data normalized

00:36:24.660 --> 00:36:26.730
and having small
access status tables.

00:36:26.730 --> 00:36:29.104
You know how you'd normally
do that in Firebase and stuff

00:36:29.104 --> 00:36:29.677
like that?

00:36:29.677 --> 00:36:31.010
So you could get extra charges--

00:36:31.010 --> 00:36:31.850
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That's
a very good point.

00:36:31.850 --> 00:36:32.640
MARK MANDEL: That way.

00:36:32.640 --> 00:36:33.470
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:36:33.470 --> 00:36:35.650
Yeah, so good to take in--
no, good to take care of that,

00:36:35.650 --> 00:36:35.890
yeah.

00:36:35.890 --> 00:36:36.640
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:36:36.640 --> 00:36:38.870
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
And we also asked

00:36:38.870 --> 00:36:45.880
one of the solution engineers
here Google for Work, Terrence,

00:36:45.880 --> 00:36:49.480
and what he said that-- he
said that indeed there was not

00:36:49.480 --> 00:36:53.010
too small, which,
again, it's not--

00:36:53.010 --> 00:36:54.170
there's nothing too small.

00:36:54.170 --> 00:36:55.350
There's nothing too big.

00:36:55.350 --> 00:36:56.020
It's BigQuery.

00:36:56.020 --> 00:36:58.390
It just handles
everything you want.

00:36:58.390 --> 00:37:01.625
But that his rule of thumb,
it's around 100 gigabytes,

00:37:01.625 --> 00:37:03.930
so if you have less than
that, it's not probably

00:37:03.930 --> 00:37:07.130
worth sharding, and over
that it starts making sense.

00:37:07.130 --> 00:37:11.050
So if you have hundreds of
megs, probably you're good.

00:37:11.050 --> 00:37:13.180
If you start having
terabytes, then that's

00:37:13.180 --> 00:37:16.020
where you should
definitely start

00:37:16.020 --> 00:37:17.500
sharding a little bit indeed.

00:37:17.500 --> 00:37:17.860
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:37:17.860 --> 00:37:18.680
No, that sounds good.

00:37:18.680 --> 00:37:20.850
And for those who aren't
as familiar with BigQuery,

00:37:20.850 --> 00:37:23.880
BigQuery has some
great tooling built in

00:37:23.880 --> 00:37:25.880
for running queries
across tables

00:37:25.880 --> 00:37:28.340
that have date formats
in them, so you

00:37:28.340 --> 00:37:30.690
don't have to be afraid of
sharding, which is nice.

00:37:30.690 --> 00:37:34.380
But it just makes it a little--
a little bit better if you're--

00:37:34.380 --> 00:37:36.440
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Actually
it may be a good idea

00:37:36.440 --> 00:37:39.100
to give a short, short
description of what BigQuery

00:37:39.100 --> 00:37:40.060
is.

00:37:40.060 --> 00:37:42.511
It's one of my favorite
Google Cloud platform products

00:37:42.511 --> 00:37:43.010
actually.

00:37:43.010 --> 00:37:43.240
MARK MANDEL: Yup.

00:37:43.240 --> 00:37:44.740
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Because, basically,

00:37:44.740 --> 00:37:47.470
it allows you to do
SQL-like requests,

00:37:47.470 --> 00:37:49.270
so if you know how to
write SQL statements,

00:37:49.270 --> 00:37:51.090
you're good, basically.

00:37:51.090 --> 00:37:53.310
But if you're going
to enter, if you're

00:37:53.310 --> 00:37:56.530
going to query
terabytes of data--

00:37:56.530 --> 00:38:00.102
instead of taking minutes or
hours, it just takes seconds.

00:38:00.102 --> 00:38:00.810
MARK MANDEL: Yeah

00:38:00.810 --> 00:38:01.976
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That's fun.

00:38:01.976 --> 00:38:04.140
MARK MANDEL: No, we had a
really good chat recently

00:38:04.140 --> 00:38:06.050
with some people about
BigQuery, and where is

00:38:06.050 --> 00:38:07.350
the right place for BigQuery.

00:38:07.350 --> 00:38:09.000
And I think that the
takeaway from that

00:38:09.000 --> 00:38:11.690
was if you have such large
datasets that it's taking you

00:38:11.690 --> 00:38:13.930
hours to process them,
BigQuery can allow you

00:38:13.930 --> 00:38:15.940
to a-- not worry
about infrastructure

00:38:15.940 --> 00:38:17.400
because we manage that for you.

00:38:17.400 --> 00:38:19.066
But take those hours
and move those down

00:38:19.066 --> 00:38:22.139
into seconds, which I
think is really powerful.

00:38:22.139 --> 00:38:23.680
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Yeah, it's basically

00:38:23.680 --> 00:38:27.520
what we use internally at
Google to query our own logs,

00:38:27.520 --> 00:38:30.470
and we do have a lot of logs.

00:38:30.470 --> 00:38:32.160
So yeah, big data.

00:38:32.160 --> 00:38:33.160
Not a problem with that.

00:38:33.160 --> 00:38:33.990
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, definitely.

